{
  "_note": "Yellow perch (Perca flavescens), a common schooling fish of Midwest lakes. Markings from field-guide descriptions: golden-yellow sides with six to eight dark vertical bars that run down from an olive back, a white belly, two separate dorsal fins (the first spiny, with a dark blotch at its rear), and orange lower fins. From above, as seen from a boat: a slim torpedo about a sixth as wide as it is long, widest under the first dorsal fin, the snout tapering to a point. The dorsal and anal fins are thin wedges and the tail a thin blade, as on a real fish, so the fins read from the side and the tail fans out as it swims (SECOND-ORDER R2.130-R2.139). 200 mm long, the base length (SECOND-ORDER R1.150-R1.169).",
